WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.100
In the basement sits
the old analog exchange,

2
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.620
and this month it gets switched off.

3
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.640
Your boss wants the new digital
line to sound exactly the same.

4
00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:09.600
The manual describes its line filter:

5
00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:10.803
a Butterworth low-pass,

6
00:00:10.823 --> 00:00:12.500
cutoff thirty-four hundred hertz,

7
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.020
where it passes seventy-one percent.

8
00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.680
A colleague's script turns
that into a digital filter,

9
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.040
the textbook recipe.

10
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.000
Next morning,
a technician plays tones through both.

11
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.320
At one thousand hertz the copy is perfect.

12
00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.940
At thirty-four hundred,
the exchange passes seventy-one percent,

13
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.310
and your copy passes ten.

14
00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.700
Customers sound dull, as if the top
of every voice was shaved off.

15
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.900
So what does the recipe do to frequencies?

16
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.940
The exchange never sampled anything.

17
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.520
Its sound is a voltage in a wire,
changing smoothly,

18
00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:40.580
through a circuit of capacitors and coils.

19
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:41.740
That's an analog filter.

20
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.020
With no samples,
there's no wheel and no limit:

21
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.680
it has a response at every frequency,

22
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.480
up to a million hertz and beyond.

23
00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:49.370
So here's the mismatch.

24
00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:52.150
Analog frequencies live on
a ruler that runs on forever.

25
00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.070
Digital tones live on half a circle,

26
00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:55.690
from zero to four thousand hertz.

27
00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.570
Copying means deciding where
each mark of the ruler lands.

28
00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.210
Wrap it round and round,
and five thousand lands on three thousand.

29
00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:02.110
That's aliasing.

30
00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:03.910
Or bend it on once, and squeeze.

31
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.340
That squeeze is the bilinear transform.

32
00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.860
Near zero,
the ruler lands almost one for one:

33
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.820
five hundred hertz lands
on four ninety-four.

34
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.200
Further up it squeezes harder,

35
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.800
and infinity lands
exactly on four thousand.

36
00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.300
That uneven squeeze is warping.

37
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.140
And thirty-four hundred lands
on twenty-three sixty-three.

38
00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.100
There's your bug, in one row.

39
00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.850
The squeeze never changes a gain.

40
00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:25.690
It only moves gains to new tones.

41
00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.650
So the copy passes seventy-one percent,

42
00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.370
exactly, at the tone where
thirty-four hundred landed.

43
00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.190
Every gain is right, at the wrong tone.

44
00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.390
And once you know where each one lands,
you can aim.

45
00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:36.450
Run the squeeze backward.

46
00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:39.430
Which analog frequency lands
on thirty-four hundred?

47
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:40.250
Forty-four hundred?

48
00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:40.910
Five thousand?

49
00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:42.850
Start at thirty-four hundred on

50
00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:44.830
the digital side, go across to the curve,

51
00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.530
then down to the analog frequency.

52
00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.550
Oh, ten thousand six hundred seven.

53
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:50.630
More than three times higher,

54
00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.410
far above anything the line can carry.

55
00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:53.390
That's prewarping.

56
00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:58.910
Build the analog prototype, the filter you
copy, with its cutoff there. Then squeeze.

57
00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:02.070
Its whole stretch up to the cutoff
lands below thirty-four hundred,

58
00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:03.450
and that stretch is nearly flat.

59
00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:05.110
The technician measures again:

60
00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.470
seventy point seven percent
at thirty-four hundred hertz.

61
00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.590
You changed one number.

62
00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:10.650
That's the whole fix.

63
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.140
Prewarping pins one tone,

64
00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.100
and every other tone is still squeezed.

65
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.380
But the cutoff is the tone worth pinning:

66
00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.580
below it, the copy never strays
more than one point six decibels.

67
00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.650
One more job moves off the exchange.

68
00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.590
A caller presses a key,
and the keypad sends two tones at once.

69
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:27.950
You get a few hundred numbers.

70
00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:28.870
Which key was it?
